1. Direct Interventions (Punitive Actions)
When the AI flags a student or the proctor sees something suspicious, they need immediate control over that student's terminal.

The "Freeze" Protocol: Instantly pause a specific student's timer and lock their screen with a "Proctor Intervention" overlay. The student cannot continue until the proctor unfreezes them.

Issue Direct Strike / Warning: Send a high-priority, flashing alert directly to the student's screen (e.g., "Please keep your eyes on the monitor" or "Disconnect the secondary display").

Force Termination (Kill Switch): Instantly boot the student from the exam, force-submit their current progress, and lock them out permanently for a severe violation.

2. Override & Support (Helpful Actions)
Students have hardware failures, internet drops, or system glitches. The proctor needs tools to rescue them without breaking the exam for everyone else.

Time Injection: Manually add extra time (e.g., +5 minutes, +10 minutes) to a specific student's clock to compensate for an internet drop.

Manual Unlock / Clearance Override: If the "Dynamic Entry Lockout" blocked a student for being late, or if the AI auto-locked them for a false positive, the proctor can click a button to manually unban their IP/Terminal and let them back in.

Force Re-Sync: If a student claims their exam froze, the proctor can force the student's client to refresh and re-sync with the database without losing their saved answers.

3. Batch Commands (Global Actions)
Actions the proctor can execute that affect everyone currently in the live matrix.

Global Broadcast: Send a top-banner message to all active terminals (e.g., "15 minutes remaining," or "Correction: Question 4 has a typo").

Mass Pause: If the testing center loses internet or there is a fire drill, the proctor can freeze the clock for the entire batch simultaneously.

Force Auto-Collect: When time is up, if some students' clients are lagging or failing to submit, the proctor can hit one button to force-pull all remaining active exams into the database.

4. Telemetry Controls (What the Proctor is Watching)
How the proctor interacts with the data feed.

Cycle Video Feeds: Switch between watching a student's webcam, their screen share, or the AI's skeletal/gaze tracking overlay.

Acknowledge/Dismiss Flags: As the AI generates anomaly flags (e.g., "Multiple faces detected"), the proctor can click "Verify Violation" (which adds a strike) or "Dismiss" (if it was just a poster on the wall).



